      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Manufacturers' claims of intellectual property protection are largely a thinly veiled attempt to maintain their monopoly on repairs. In reality, the laws surrounding intellectual property are often misused to stifle competition and limit consumer choice. The Digital Millennium Copyright Act (DMCA) and other intellectual property laws can be used to restrict access to repair information, but these laws were not designed to protect manufacturers' profits at the expense of consumers. In fact, many experts argue that the DMCA's anti-circumvention provisions have been used to stifle innovation and limit access to repair information, rather than promoting legitimate intellectual property protection.",
      "source": "target",
